<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gustav Noske</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gustav Noske was a German Social Democratic politician. As people's commissioner and then minister of defence in 1919–1920, he used the army and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> to crush the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacist-uprising">Spartacist uprising</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> Soviet Republic and other left-wing risings, earning lasting hatred on the left. He resigned after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapp-putsch">Kapp Putsch</a> of 1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gustav-noske.jpg" alt="Gustav Noske" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Noske_gustav_before1918.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gustav Noske was born on 9 July 1868 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-an-der-havel">Brandenburg an der Havel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, the son of a weaver and a manual labourer. He was apprenticed as a basket maker and travelled as a journeyman to Halle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Liegnitz</a>. He joined the Social Democratic Party in 1884, and in 1892 he became chairman of the Brandenburg SPD. From 1902 to 1918 he was chief editor of the Volksstimme in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemnitz">Chemnitz</a>, and from 1907 he sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a>.</p><p>He was the party's expert on military, navy and colonial questions and a reformist. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he supported the war loans and, in 1916 to 1918, spoke for a commission that investigated military procurement and excess profits.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In early November 1918 Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-maximilian-of-baden">Max von Baden</a> sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a> to negotiate an end to the mutiny. The mutineers elected him chairman of the Soldiers' Council, but within days he had restored the officers' authority. On 30 December 1918 he joined the Council of the People's Deputies, responsible for military affairs. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacist-uprising">Spartacist uprising</a> began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 5 January 1919, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert">Friedrich Ebert</a> ordered it quashed and Noske used regular forces and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> units, and around 165 people died. On 15 January Freikorps men under Captain Waldemar Pabst abducted and murdered Karl Liebknecht and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg">Rosa Luxemburg</a>. Noske had earlier ordered Liebknecht's telephone line monitored and reported to Pabst.</p><p>In February 1919 he became Reichswehrminister in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philipp-scheidemann">Philipp Scheidemann</a>'s government, and he stayed in Gustav Bauer's. Over the first half of 1919 left-wing uprisings were put down by army and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> across the Reich. He supported signing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> despite misgivings. On 28 February 1920 he dissolved two Freikorps naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> on the orders of the Allied commission, and General Walther von Lüttwitz's refusal led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapp-putsch">Kapp Putsch</a>. When the unions demanded his resignation as a condition for ending the general strike, he resigned on 22 March 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Noske was governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-hanover">Province of Hanover</a> from 1920. The Nazi government dismissed him on 1 October 1933. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> arrested him on 22 July 1944 on suspicion of involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/20-july-plot">20 July plot</a>, and he spent seven months in camps, among them Ravensbrück. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> on 30 November 1946 from a stroke. He remains controversial. Some historians credit him with the courage to be, in his own words, the bloodhound who spared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> chaos, while another called him a primitive brute in love with violence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gustav Noske” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 4 February 1919 the Freikorps Division Gerstenberg, sent by <b>Gustav Noske</b>, stormed Bremen.</blockquote><a href="/state/bremen-soviet-republic">Bremen Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-an-der-havel">Brandenburg an der Havel</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.41,12.55&amp;year=1918" aria-label="Brandenburg an der Havel on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4832 355 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gustav Noske"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5282 625h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5282 625h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.41,12.55&amp;year=1918">What was here in 1918? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
